    "lawCode": "Amd §1005, Pub Auth L",
    "lawSection": "Public Authorities Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S5126\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend the public authorities law, in\nrelation to the authority to contract for energy\n\n PURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL :\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to ensure that the New York Power\nAuthority (NYPA) does not encourage the development of transmission\nlines that discriminate against electric generating facilities in New\nYork state by contracting to acquire energy, or environmental\nattributes associated with energy, that is generated by electric\ngenerating facilities located outside of the country and is delivered\nover transmission lines that do not provide access to electric\ngenerating facilities in New York to transmit their energy within New\nYork State.\n\n SPECIFIC PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1 of this bill would apply to energy transmitted over an\nelectric transmission line that: (1) may be, but is not limited to, a\nhigh voltage direct current electric transmission line; (2) enters\ncommercial operation after the effective date of this act; (3)\nconnects a location outside of the Unites States to a location within\nNew York State; and (4) does not provide access within New York State\nfor the state's electric generating facilities also to transmit their\nenergy over the line.\n\nSection 1 of the bill would prohibit NYPA from contracting for or\notherwise buying or acquiring energy, or environmental attributes\nassociated with energy transmitted over such an electric transmission\nline, from any company, any municipal entity, or any other legal\nentity.\n\nSection 2 of the bill provides for an immediate effective date of\nthese provisions.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nThe ability of NYPA to enter into contracts involves the use of its\nbuying power and leverage to command the kinds of goods and services\nthat it desires. NYPA's economic strength should be used to send\nsignals to markets to help achieve a public purpose that benefits New\nYork State as a whole and is in the public interest.\n\nNYPA currently has a contract with the City of New York to supply its\npower needs. Among its other customers, in addition to the City of New\nYork, NYPA's statute also requires it to supply power and energy for\nthe Metropolitan Transportation Authority, its subsidiary\ncorporations, the New York City Transit Authority, the Port Authority\nof New York and New Jersey, the State of New York, the United States,\nother public corporations and electric corporations within the\nmetropolitan area of the City of New York. The New York City\nAdministrative Code requires the City of New York to contract for or\notherwise purchase or acquire hydroelectric or other forms of energy\nas shall be available from NYPA, among other entities.\n\nIn 1989, NYPA entered into a contract with Hydro-Quebec, an electric\nutility wholly owned by the Province of Quebec, Canada, for the\nimportation of 1,000 megawatts (MW) of capacity. NYPA also entered\ninto contracts with three New York State utilities and its\ngovernmental customers in Downstate New York for the resale of the\npower. In 1992, NYPA cancelled its 1989 contract because the\ncontracted price was too expensive.\n\n\nIn September of 2016, the Office of Sustainability under New York City\nMayor Bill de Blasio issued its \"Roadmap to 80 x 50,\" which\nacknowledges that the City issued its 2015 Renewable Energy Request\nfor Information. The document noted that the City is speaking with\nproject developers seeking to import renewable energy from outside the\nCity via high voltage direct current transmission lines.\n\nTransmission lines that originate outside the territorial limits of\nthe United States and that do not provide access to electric\ngenerating facilities in New York to transmit energy within New York\nState do not sufficiently encourage employment opportunities or\neconomic development within the state, and such transmission lines\nharm electric generating facilities in New York. Such lines would\nbypass existing and new electric generating facilities in New York\nState, including renewable energy resources. More specifically, such\ntransmission projects will: (1) discourage the building of new\ngenerating facilities, especially renewable energy resources, in New\nYork State and transmission lines to transmit energy generated in New\nYork state to where it is needed; (2) discourage the repowering of\nexisting generating facilities within New York State to reduce\nemissions further in this state; and (3) discourage the rebuilding of\nexisting transmission lines in the state to transmit energy from\nUpstate to Downstate. If NYPA, either directly or on behalf of the\nCity of New York, were to enter into a contract to buy power that\nwould come over such transmission lines, the contract would provide\nthe huge subsidy from NYPA's ratepayers (and likely the City's\ntaxpayers) that would be needed for such transmission lines to be\nbuilt.\n\nConstruction and development of transmission lines that provide access\nto electric generating facilities in New York State maximizes\nemployment and economic development in New York State and in our\nnation. As the United States moves toward supporting procurement that\nfavors American-made resources at both the national and state levels,\nNYPA's contracting power should not be used to enable projects that\nwould import power from another country when greater value is derived\nby investing in New York's own electric system and the economic\nbenefits, tax revenues, and jobs those generation and transmission\nresources provide to their local communities. New York State cannot\nafford to send those jobs and revenues to Canada, and such an action\nwould not be in the public interest.\n\n PRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nNew Bill\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
